      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "maj7",
      "function_role": "zone-anchor-for-harmonic-minor",
      "narrative": "In Chapter 3, the Ionian (#5) scale -- the relative major of harmonic minor -- anchors the five-zone fretboard system via the Eb+(maj7) guide chord shape. Benson writes: 'Guide Chords => Harmonic Minor & Ionian (#5) = Cm(maj7) & Ebmaj7(#5)' (Chapter 3, p. 238).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "five-zone-guide-chord-navigation",
        "ionian-sharp5-scale"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-4-approach-tones",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-4-approach-tones",
          "citation": "Chapter 3 p. 238"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "dorian-static-scale-superimposition",
      "narrative": "Benson establishes that the Dorian scale is a 'static scale, which means that it does not have any avoid note. Therefore, we can play all the tetrads and triads from the harmonic field over a dorian chord' -- listing all seven diatonic tetrads and triads of the parent key (Chapter 2, p. 144).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "dorian-scale",
        "ii-v-superimposition",
        "parent-key-tetrads",
        "parent-key-triads"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-4-approach-tones",
