      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "dorian-with-added-major7-leading-tone",
      "narrative": "Benson prescribes adding the major 7th to the Dorian scale as a leading tone: 'we can add the major 7th to the dorian scale, in order to make it more melodic. The reason we can do this, is because the major 7th works as a leading tone to the root of the scale, thus creating the illusion of a dominant 7th chord leading to a minor chord' (Chapter 2, p. 144).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "major7-leading-tone-addition",
        "dorian-with-major7"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-4-approach-tones",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-4-approach-tones",
          "citation": "Chapter 2 p. 144"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "d",
      "granularity_name": "color-tone-policy"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "dorian-as-mandatory-color-over-non-diatonic",
      "narrative": "Benson distinguishes two tiers: 'We can use dorian ideas over any minor chord as a possibility of color. However, when we have minor chords that do not belong to the harmonic field, there is no other option than using dorian ideas, otherwise the melody will sound wrong' (Chapter 2, p. 144). Dorian is optional over diatonic minor chords but mandatory over non-diatonic ones.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "dorian-scale",
        "ii-v-superimposition"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-4-approach-tones",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "method-vol-4-approach-tones",
          "citation": "Chapter 2 p. 144"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "dorian-lines-universal-across-all-parent-modes",
      "narrative": "Benson generalizes: 'all the melodic lines in this chapter can be used over any chord derived from C major scale: Dm7 (Dorian), Em7 or Esus(b9) (Phrygian), Fmaj7 (Lydian), G7 (Mixolydian), Am7 (Aeolian), Bm7b5 (Locrian)' (Chapter 2, p. 180).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "dorian-universalizes-to-all-parent-modes",
        "ii-v-superimposition"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "method-vol-4-approach-tones",
      "references": [
        {
